1. INTERVIEW READY ANSWERS
======================================================================

Q1. Why was Linked List introduced?

Answer:
Linked List was introduced to overcome the limitations of arrays.

Problems with Arrays
• Fixed size
• Memory wastage
• Costly insertion and deletion due to shifting

Linked List provides
• Dynamic memory allocation
• Efficient insertion and deletion
• No fixed size limitation

------------------------------------------------------------

Q2. What is a Linked List?

Answer:
A Linked List is a linear data structure consisting of nodes. Each node contains data and a reference (pointer) to the next node. Nodes are stored in non-contiguous memory locations and are connected using references.

------------------------------------------------------------

Q3. Array vs Linked List

Feature               Array                    Linked List

Memory                Contiguous               Non-Contiguous

Size                  Fixed                    Dynamic

Random Access         O(1)                     O(n)

Insertion             O(n)                     O(1)*

Deletion              O(n)                     O(1)*

Memory                Less                     More

*After reaching the required position.

------------------------------------------------------------

Q4. Why doesn't Linked List support Random Access?

Answer:
Linked List nodes are stored in non-contiguous memory locations. Since the address of the next node is known only through the current node's reference, we must traverse the list from the head until the required node is reached.

Time Complexity

Access
→ O(n)

------------------------------------------------------------

Q5. Advantages of Linked List

Answer:
• Dynamic size
• Memory allocated only when required
• Efficient insertion
• Efficient deletion
• No memory wastage due to fixed size

------------------------------------------------------------

Q6. Disadvantages of Linked List

Answer:
• No random access
• O(n) traversal for access/search
• Extra memory for reference pointer
• Not cache friendly

------------------------------------------------------------

Q7. Time Complexity of Operations

Operation                          Time

Access by Index                    O(n)

Search                             O(n)

Insert at Beginning                O(1)

Insert at End (Head Only)          O(n)

Insert at End (Head + Tail)        O(1)

Delete at Beginning                O(1)

Delete at End                      O(n)

------------------------------------------------------------

Q8. Why is insertion at the beginning O(1)?

Answer:
The head node is directly available. A new node is created, its next pointer is set to the current head, and the head is updated. No traversal is required.

------------------------------------------------------------

Q9. Why is insertion at an arbitrary position O(n)?

Answer:
Before inserting, we must traverse from the head to the required position. Traversal takes O(n), while updating references takes O(1).

------------------------------------------------------------

Q10. Why does Linked List require extra memory?

Answer:
Every node stores:
• Data
• Reference (Next Pointer)

The additional reference occupies extra memory for every node, making Linked Lists less memory-efficient than arrays.

